  "text": "**Solution 1** gives $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}$, the upper endpoint of the admissible interval. That endpoint is not a threshold inside the interval, so this is wrong.\n\n**Solution 2** gives $q^* = 1 + \\frac{\\alpha+2}{N} = \\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N}$. The mass-scaling computation is right: under $u_\\lambda = \\lambda^{N/2}u(\\lambda x)$ the Choquard term scales as $\\lambda^{Np-N-\\alpha}$, and the $L^2$-critical balance $Np - N - \\alpha = 2$ gives this value. The justification for the threshold is loose, but the final answer is correct.\n\n**Solution 3** gives $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N}$ by the same mass-critical argument, and it states the threshold more clearly. It also checks that this value lies in $\\left(\\frac{N+\\alpha}{N}, \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}\\right)$. That check holds because $(N+\\alpha+2)(N-2) < N(N+\\alpha)$ reduces to $\\alpha > -2$. The answer is correct.\n\n**Solution 4** answers $2/3$ for a sumset/Sidon-set question that has nothing to do with the Choquard equation, so it is off-target.\n\nSolutions 2 and 3 agree on the correct value $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N}$. I choose Solution 3 because its threshold argument is the more coherent of the two.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
